 able to strike at will  they say the taliban aren't hardline  enough and want to establish an islamic  ruled empire across afghanistan pakistan  and parts of iran  terrorizing ethnic groups like the  hazara people is just one of their  tactics  but it's effective  i get scared that a suicide attack may  occur  because our people are directly under  the threat of isis  whether the taliban can stop isis  attacks is a key test for the government  if they fail civil war could result and  the country could again become a haven  for militants looking to strike the west  for more on this story i'm joined by  journalist ali latifi ali can you give  us an idea of the overall security  situation right now  the overall security situation  afghanistan you know the issue is that  the taliban had promised that once they  come to power the war will come to an  end  and people will no longer have to fear  things like suicide bombings and  targeted killings and  ieds and you know just sort of these  things that that keep people from living  their daily lives and what we've seen  over the last three four months is that  that hasn't really happened um yes  large-scale suicide attacks and things  like that are down considerably compared  to you know the previous 20 years but  they're still not at zero and we still  have you know these so-called daish  forces the so-called islamic state  forces that are posing threats and still  carrying out really heinous really  large-scale attacks that are you know  just as brutal as they used to be over  the last five six years and then you  also have places where there's a lot of  issues with targeted killings where  people feel as if they could be killed  or kidnapped or targeted  or abused in some way  and a lot of them will say like for  instance i was talking to people in the  northern city of mazar you know people  who had been involved in um  human rights who had been involved in  women's rights who had been involved  with the security forces with politics  with certain legal things and they were  saying in a lot of cases it's not even  that we feel that it's the the taliban  that are targeting us it could really be  anybody it could be criminals it could  be mafias it could be ish anything else  but in that because of given you know  what some of these people did they don't  feel comfortable going to the taliban  for assistance because they feel like  the taliban  may find out you know for instance that  they were to  jail domestic abusers or that they you  know worked in the legal system or that  they're part of the security forces and  hold that against them  in some other way so security is still  very much an issue that the taliban have  yet to really figure out  now ali you mentioned that the taliban  has repeatedly promised to provide  security does the group have the  capabilities to handle this type of  insurgency though  this is exactly the question that people  are asking right is is that you know so  far they haven't been able to  because we've seen attacks in kandao  we've seen attacks in kunduz in uh  in in the east and in jalalabad and in  the districts of ningahara we've seen it  in kanad we've seen it in kabul we've  seen fighting in the city of mazar  between taliban and daesh so so far no  they haven't been able to  establish this sense of security and  that's going to be really important for  them going forward because that was  supposed like the two things that people  thought that at the very least of the  taliban come to power that you know we  would no longer have to worry about one  would be corruption and the other would  be security and so far the security part  has not been  all that successful ali let's talk about  the regional actors they all have a  vested interest in a stable afghanistan  india and pakistan are holding separate  summits what do the people of  afghanistan actually need from delhi  islamabad and others what needs to be  done to help them right now  i mean 